# Page Objects and components

## Ownership

- A **Page Object** owns one page, screen, route or navigation boundary: route beats visual placement — an
  area with its own URL or menu entry gets its own Page Object (`src/pages/<app>/<name>.page.ts`); a block
  that is part of an existing page's layout never gets one.
- A **component** owns one meaningful block inside a page (§When a component). Page Objects compose
  components; tests reach a component through its page (`ordersPage.filters.choose("Open")`).
- Wired in `src/fixtures/<app>/pages.fixture.ts`; specs take Page Objects from fixtures, never
  `new XPage(page)` (`qa:gate`, UI specs) — E2E may, on a second role's own browser context.
- Neither orchestrates a flow across pages, knows unrelated pages or a whole journey, nor becomes a domain
  service: cross-page flows are the spec's, each page through its own Page Object.

## What it holds — and nothing else

**Locators** (readonly fields, §Locators), **the components it is made of**, **actions** a person takes,
**readers** of what the screen shows, as shown, and structural markers. Both answer in the case's words:
- an action of the case is **one method** — `orders.expand(order)` finds the row and expands it; the test
  does not chain `search`, `row` and `expand`;
- a block a test checks has **one reader**, `read()`, returning the block as one object or list shaped like
  its expectation `shown<Block>(…)` (`specs.mdc` §A UI test reads as its case). Which of two toggles is on is
  a reader too (`selectedView()` → "Completed"); data components return what is shown
  (`results.names()`, `results.prices()`).

An action ends when the screen is ready for the next one: the content or its empty state is shown, a spinner
is gone. The address is not that sign: on a client-side route (`#/active`, a router push) it changes before the
screen does — wait for what the screen shows (the tab shown selected, the new heading), never `waitForURL` before
a read (the gate warns).

| Not a Page Object's job | Its home |
|---|---|
| `expect`, `test.step`, hooks, screenshots, test-runner logic, reporting metadata | the spec |
| test data (builders, generators, defaults, random values), API setup, `process.env` | `src/data/`, fixtures |
| cross-page flows, another route's locators | the spec, each page through its own Page Object |
| listening to the network (`waitForResponse`, `on("request")`, `captureRequests`) | the spec with `captureRequests` / `whenAnswered` (`src/setup/request-capture.ts`); request patterns in `src/setup/<app>/` |
| mocking a response (`page.route`) | the spec, under `// mocked:` |
| preparing the browser (`addInitScript`, storage, cookies, `page.evaluate`) | `src/setup/<app>/`; an `open…()` may call it |
| turning shown text into data or back (dates, money) | the app's display helper `src/assertions/<app>/display.ts` |
| any export besides the class and the types its methods take | the home of what it is |

- **The one network wait allowed**: when the screen gives no sign that new data arrived (figures change in
  place), the action waits for the answers that bring it — `whenAnswered(this.page, paths, () => …)`, never a
  hand-written `waitForResponse`, and only for an action that surely asks the server.
- **No state**: every field `readonly`, set once; no stored answer, counter or flag.
- `page` is `protected`: a spec never reaches past the Page Object (`ordersPage.page.getByRole(…)`); a spec
  that needs the browser itself takes the `page` fixture.
- **Method design**: explicit, user intent, only what current tests use — no
  `performAdvancedSortingWorkflowWithValidation()`, no `sortByRating()` "for later", never an action and a
  check in one method (`sortByPriceDescAndVerify()`).

## Opening a page

- Addresses are relative to the app's own, never from the host's root: `goto("./")`, `goto("orders/42")` — an app
  under a path (`https://host/shop`) keeps it (`urlOf` gives its base the trailing slash); `goto("/orders")` would
  open the host's root (the gate fails it).
- After navigating, `await appReady(this.page)` (`src/setup/app-wide.ts`) — the app's "loaded" sign from the
  map's ```app-wide block (`ready when: hidden …`). Overlays the app shows on its own (a cookie banner,
  "what's new", "session expired") are closed there, on every page: add a `dismiss:` line to the map, never
  handle them in a spec or Page Object (`addLocatorHandler` elsewhere fails the gate). A toast is never such
  an overlay — it is the app's answer to an action and the test checks it.
- `open()` waits for "content **or** empty state", never one only. A loaded or empty-state marker holds for
  every viewer and data state: check it live for each role the plan uses and both data states; match every
  variant (`getByText(/(You have|This customer has) no orders yet/)`), naming them in a comment.
- A move that opens a new tab (`target="_blank"`) returns the new tab's Page Object and the test continues
  there; not under test → open the target's address in its own tab instead:

  ```ts
  async openCustomerProfile(): Promise<ProfilePage> {
    const popup = this.page.waitForEvent("popup");
    await this.customerLink.click();
    const tab = await popup;
    await appReady(tab);
    return new ProfilePage(tab);
  }
  ```

- Never another route's locators: after navigating from A to B, B's markers come from B's Page Object.

## Pages built alike share a base

Pages of one kind (reports with one period picker, settings tabs) differ in what they show, not how they open.
Their base opens them, goes through the menu and works the shared controls; each page **declares** what
differs and copies no method:

```ts
export abstract class ReportPage extends AppPage {
  protected abstract readonly address: string;
  protected abstract readonly menuItem: string;
  protected abstract readonly loadsFrom: readonly string[];

  async open(): Promise<void> {
    await whenAnswered(this.page, this.loadsFrom, () => this.page.goto(this.address));
    await appReady(this.page);
  }
}

export class SalesReportPage extends ReportPage {
  protected readonly address = "reports/sales";
  protected readonly menuItem = "Sales";
  protected readonly loadsFrom = ["/api/reports/sales"];
}
```

The base reads declarations only in its methods, never its constructor. What a page still draws after its
data arrives goes in one `protected async ready()` hook the base calls. A page that opens differently
overrides that one method and says why. A public method written again in two siblings is the base's.

## When a component

A block becomes a component for one of **three reasons** — by meaning, not by count:
1. **It appears on 2+ pages or routes** (a header and its search, a side menu, a shared dialog or picker):
   one copy in `src/components/<app>/`.
2. **It repeats on its page**, each copy with parts of its own (a card per item, a row with its buttons): one
   component per copy — `ordersPage.card(name).editButton`.
3. **It is a self-contained block with behaviour of its own** (its own root, actions or readers: a dialog, a
   table you search and read, a widget you work) and keeping it would push the Page Object past **~15
   locators or ~150 statements**.

- Never size alone (it is the sign to look for such a block — the gate warns past it), never a root locator
  alone, never because a block exists visually. A group of plain fields with no actions of its own (a form tab
  of four inputs) stays in its owner under a comment naming the group; a block used only inside one other
  component stays part of it; a wrapper renaming a Playwright call never qualifies; no component per field.
- **Look before creating**: search `src/components/<app>/` and Page Objects holding such a block; extend what
  exists. **One component per kind**: blocks differing only in an optional part are one component whose
  readers cover both.
- **It lives inside its root**: it takes its root (or the container and title that find it), every locator
  starts from the root, and what the library draws outside it (a picker's list, a tooltip) is reached
  through `this.root.page()`. It keeps no state. Past ~15 locators / ~150 statements, a part of it with
  behaviour of its own becomes its own component; plain fields stay.
- **App-wide parts** (header, side menu) are reached alike from every Page Object — a shared base Page Object
  or a `layout` property every page gets — never re-created per page. A sign-out through them needs a session
  of its own (`specs.mdc` §Signing in).
- **Several apps on one design system**: a design-system component lives once in a library-named folder
  (`src/components/design-system/`) and every app's Page Objects use it — locators inside a root the Page
  Object passes in, no `goto`, no app's own block. Page Objects, clients and builders always stay under their
  app.
- **The plan names them** before code: the UI brief's `Components: reuse <Name> · new <Name> (reason 1 | 2 | 3)`.
- Naming by role, not a forced template: structural (header, sidebar, nav), control (sort, filter,
  pagination, search), data (list, table, grid — readers return what is shown), overlay (dialog, dropdown;
  toasts need none — `expectToast` finds them by text; overlays the app shows on its own go to the map), form
  (grouped inputs). A new kind of block: name it after its real role; never force it into a category.

## Locators

`getByTestId` when the element has one → `getByRole` with its accessible name → `getByLabel` →
`getByPlaceholder` → `getByText` (when stable) → CSS as a last resort.
- The test id attribute is the app's own: the ```app-wide `test id:` line in the map (`data-testid` without one),
  given to its projects by `playwright.config.ts`; check it live. `getByTestId` reads it — never hand-roll
  `locator('[data-testid="…"]')`. No test id → never invent one;
  take the next option, and when it is fragile note the missing test id in the plan's change log (a finding
  for the product team, not a code comment).
- No XPath, `nth-child`, generated class names or layout-dependent selectors without a stated reason.
  Locators come from the live app (`live-app.mdc`) — never guessed, never asked of the user — and live only in
  Page Objects and components; a spec reads as intent (`await loginPage.signIn(customer)`).
- Every locator and component is a typed `readonly` field, one line each in the constructor
  (`this.email = page.getByTestId("email")`, `this.banner = new Banner(page.getByRole("banner"))`). A locator
  others start from (the main area) is a field set above them (`this.main`), not a repeated local. A subclass
  calls `super(page)` first, then sets its own fields. One name per locator: no field set from another
  (`this.months = this.axisLabels`).
- **The one parameterized locator**: a method taking a parameter only when the element has no role, label,
  placeholder or test id and only spec-supplied data finds it — exactly that one locator, no action or check,
  never a way around a missing test id:

  ```ts
  orderName(name: string): Locator { return this.main.getByText(name, { exact: true }); } // good — only the test's name finds it
  submitButton(label: string): Locator { return this.page.getByRole("button", { name: label }); } // bad — a selectable element made a method
  ```

## Who checks what

- Page Objects and components return what the screen shows; the spec decides what it means, comparing a
  reader with an expectation from `src/assertions/<app>/`:
  `expect(await order.delivery.read(), "Delivery details").toEqual(shownDelivery(placedOrder))`.
- A rule the shown figures follow (each row adds up) is one named check in `src/assertions/<app>/`:
  `expectRowsAddUp(await totals.figures())`; sorting and filtering: `code.mdc`. An assertion helper computes
  from what the spec already has — it never sends a request, calls a Page Object, changes data or hides a step.
- After a navigation the destination is checked through its own Page Object, never the page it came from or
  a raw locator. A step checks only facts proving this case; a heading the case does not name is not checked
  in every step.
- **What the app says after an action** (a toast, snackbar or alert after a save, delete or send — the plan's
  `expect` and UI brief `Messages:`): `expectToast(page, "<exact text>")` (`src/assertions/shared/toast.ts`) on
  the next line after the action — it closes itself within seconds — and then what the action left (the row,
  the changed value); the message alone never passes the case. Never close it, wait it out or check that it
  disappears; a failed UI test's report quotes every message the app showed.
- **Visual masks**: a Page Object exposes `readonly visualMaskTargets = [this.emailInput, this.phoneInput]` for
  the plan-backed fields only; the spec passes them as `mask:`; a reusable mask helper lives in
  `src/assertions/<app>/` (`specs.mdc` §Visual checkpoints).

## Ownership decisions and refactoring

- **A refactor only on request**: the gate warning about a Page Object's size, siblings repeating their base or a
  repeat is fixed by a behaviour-preserving refactor the user asks for — test names, intent, steps, checks, tags
  and fixture contracts unchanged, no new abstraction while removing one, failing tests healed first. A component
  split only for one of the three reasons of §When a component; a simplification by the cleanup triggers of
  `code.mdc` §When an abstraction earns its cost. Then the impacted specs with `--retries=0` and `npm run qa:gate`.
- Unclear which Page Object or component owns a block → decide per candidate: **keep** in the Page Object ·
  **extract** a component now (one of the three reasons) · **reuse** an existing component · **postpone**
  until reuse or complexity appears · **remove** an unnecessary abstraction. A component fixture only as a
  rare, explained exception; discovery is never an excuse for a large refactoring.
- Refactoring a Page Object into components — never by guesswork: first name the owner, its route or screen
  boundary, the repeated locator and action groups, the affected specs and the smallest verification
  command. Behaviour unchanged; move only related locators and methods,
  update the owner's composition, imports and affected specs minimally; no new scenarios, features,
  speculative methods, fixtures without need, generic Playwright wrappers, flows or scenario checks moved in;
  public names kept unless needed; delete what moved — no old copy beside the new component.
- Verify: the smallest impacted spec first (`npm run test:app -- <app> ui <spec> --retries=0`), the relevant UI
  specs when the component is shared, then `npm run qa:gate`; not run → say what to run and why.

## Before accepting UI code

None of: a component fixture without a reuse need; a scenario check in a Page Object or component; a
speculative or unused member; an undocumented fragile locator; a changing field; network capture, browser
preparation or value conversion in a Page Object; a page copying its base's `open()`; two components for one
kind of block; a Page Object grown into a dumping ground; a block read in pieces the test joins; an action of
the case split into calls the test chains.
